Feature: Refactor RDD-based PySpark script to DataFrame API for Databricks Runtime 15.3+

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file" exists
    And the table has a single column "value" of type string, nullable
    And the value for rows_skip is 3

  Scenario: Happy path - Skip first 3 rows using DataFrame API
    Given the table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file" contains at least 4 rows
    When the PySpark code reads the table as a DataFrame
    And the code skips the first 3 rows using DataFrame API (not RDD)
    Then the resulting DataFrame contains all rows except the first 3
    And the resulting DataFrame has the same schema as the source ("value": string, nullable)
    And no logging information is captured

  Scenario Outline: Error path - Table or column issues
    Given the table "<table_name>" does not exist or is inaccessible
    When the PySpark code attempts to read the table
    Then an error is raised with message "Table <table_name> not found or inaccessible"

    Examples:
      | table_name                                      |
      | purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file   |
      | purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.missing_table |

  Scenario Outline: Error path - Missing or invalid column
    Given the table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file" exists
    And the column "<column_name>" is missing or has wrong type
    When the PySpark code attempts to read and process the column
    Then an error is raised with message "Column <column_name> missing or invalid type"

    Examples:
      | column_name |
      | value       |
      | val         |

  Scenario: Error path - Table has fewer than 4 rows
    Given the table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file" contains fewer than 4 rows
    When the PySpark code skips the first 3 rows
    Then the resulting DataFrame is empty

  Scenario Outline: Error path - rows_skip is not an integer or negative
    Given the value for rows_skip is "<rows_skip>"
    When the PySpark code attempts to skip rows
    Then an error is raised with message "rows_skip must be a non-negative integer"

    Examples:
      | rows_skip |
      | -1        |
      | abc       |
      | null      |

  Scenario: Error path - Duplicate rows in source table
    Given the table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file" contains duplicate values in "value"
    When the PySpark code skips the first 3 rows
    Then the resulting DataFrame contains all rows except the first 3, including duplicates

  Scenario: Error path - Null values in source table
    Given the table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file" contains nulls in "value"
    When the PySpark code skips the first 3 rows
    Then the resulting DataFrame contains all rows except the first 3, including nulls

  Scenario: Validation - Output DataFrame schema
    Given the resulting DataFrame after skipping rows
    Then the DataFrame has exactly one column "value" of type string, nullable

  Scenario: Validation - No logging information captured
    Given the PySpark code is executed
    Then no log information is captured or output

  Scenario: Example PySpark code for DBR 15.3+ (no RDD, no logging)
    Given the requirement to skip first 3 rows from "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file"
    When the PySpark code is written using DataFrame API
    Then the code is as follows:
      """
      from pyspark.sql import SparkSession
      from pyspark.sql.functions import monotonically_increasing_id

      spark = SparkSession.getActiveSession()
      source_df = spark.table("purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.actual_file")
      target_df = source_df.withColumn("row_num", monotonically_increasing_id()) \
                           .orderBy("row_num") \
                           .filter("row_num >= 3") \
                           .drop("row_num")
      # Output is target_df; no logging
      """

  Scenario Outline: Representative data cases for skipping rows
    Given the table contains the following rows:
      | value         |
      | <row1>        |
      | <row2>        |
      | <row3>        |
      | <row4>        |
      | <row5>        |
    When the PySpark code skips the first 3 rows
    Then the resulting DataFrame contains:
      | value         |
      | <row4>        |
      | <row5>        |

    Examples:
      | row1   | row2   | row3   | row4   | row5   |
      | A      | B      | C      | D      | E      |
      | null   | X      | Y      | Z      | W      |
      | 1      | 2      | 3      | 4      | 5      |

# END
